{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01VT37yy9BvpQGoV8uSFkuQ1","type":"tool_result","content":"24\t// Translation of c_src/src/main.c\n25\t//\n26\t// C original:\n27\t//     int main() {\n28\t//         printf(\"Hello World!\\n\");\n29\t//         return 0;\n30\t//     }\n31\t\n32\tuse std::io::Write;\n33\tuse std::process::ExitCode;\n34\t\n35\t// The Rust runtime sets SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN before `main` runs, which a C\n36\t// program does not do. Without restoring the default disposition, writing to a\n37\t// pipe with no reader makes this program ignore the EPIPE error and exit 0,\n38\t// whereas the C program is killed by SIGPIPE (shell-visible status 141).\n39\t// Restore SIG_DFL so the observable behavior matches the C original.\n40\t#[cfg(unix)]\n41\tfn restore_default_sigpipe() {\n42\t    const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13; // Linux and macOS\n43\t    const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;\n44\t    extern \"C\" {\n45\t        fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize;\n46\t    }\n47\t    unsafe {\n48\t        signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL);\n49\t    }\n50\t}\n51\t\n52\t#[cfg(not(unix))]\n53\tfn restore_default_sigpipe() {}\n54\t\n55\tfn main() -> ExitCode {\n56\t    restore_default_sigpipe();\n57\t\n58\t    // printf(\"Hello World!\\n\");\n59\t    let stdout = std::io::stdout();\n60\t    let mut out = stdout.lock();\n61\t    // Write raw bytes so the output is byte-identical to C's printf.\n62\t    // Write errors are discarded: C's printf/exit-time flush failure likewise\n63\t    // does not change the value returned from main.\n64\t    let _ = out.write_all(b\"Hello World!\\n\");\n65\t    let _ = out.flush();\n66\t\n67\t    // return 0;\n68\t    ExitCode::from(0)\n69\t}\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96fa301f-8316-4e55-8065-0af104d8f052","uuid":"7c73eff5-47ef-4a91-8e43-82afa2193bab","timestamp":"2026-08-28T07:54:34.614Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-86yoAX/translation/src/main.rs","content":"// Translation of c_src/src/main.c\n//\n// C original:\n//     int main() {\n//         printf(\"Hello World!\\n\");\n//         return 0;\n//     }\n\nuse std::io::Write;\nuse std::process::ExitCode;\n\n// The Rust runtime sets SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN before `main` runs, which a C\n// program does not do. Without restoring the default disposition, writing to a\n// pipe with no reader makes this program ignore the EPIPE error and exit 0,\n// whereas the C program is killed by SIGPIPE (shell-visible status 141).\n// Restore SIG_DFL so the observable behavior matches the C original.\n#[cfg(unix)]\nfn restore_default_sigpipe() {\n    const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13; // Linux and macOS\n    const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;\n    extern \"C\" {\n        fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize;\n    }\n    unsafe {\n        signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL);\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(not(unix))]\nfn restore_default_sigpipe() {}\n\nfn main() -> ExitCode {\n    restore_default_sigpipe();\n\n    // printf(\"Hello World!\\n\");\n    let stdout = std::io::stdout();\n    let mut out = stdout.lock();\n    // Write raw bytes so the output is byte-identical to C's printf.\n    // Write errors are discarded: C's printf/exit-time flush failure likewise\n    // does not change the value returned from main.\n    let _ = out.write_all(b\"Hello World!\\n\");\n    let _ = out.flush();\n\n    // return 0;\n    ExitCode::from(0)\n}\n","numLines":47,"startLine":24,"totalLines":70}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_4f63ix4bz2vygjbofzfwvsr4qk4cur53icntq4g7nyfovme75tca","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. All four phases complete and verified.\n\n## What the C program is\n\n```c\nint main() { printf(\"Hello World!\\n\"); return 0; }\n```\n\nNo parameters, no stdin reads, no conditionals, one `return`. So there is **no data-dependent branching** to enumerate — the real input classes are properties of the process environment: argv shape, stdin shape, and whether stdout is writable. I enumerated 21 of them.\n\n## Phase A — builds\n\n- C: `cd c_src/build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `c_src/build/driver`\n- Rust: `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver`\n\nBoth built clean (0 warnings). No compile errors to fix.\n\n## The one real mismatch found\n\n**SIGPIPE.** With stdout on a pipe that has no reader:\n\n| | exit status |\n|---|---|\n| C | **killed by signal 13** |\n| Rust (before) | **exit(0)** |\n\nRust's std sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`; C inherits `SIG_DFL`. The failing write returned `Err(EPIPE)`, which the translation discarded, so it exited 0 instead of dying. **stdout is empty in both cases** — a stdout-only test passes right over this; only the exit status differs.\n\nFixed in `src/main.rs` by restoring `SIG_DFL` via a bare `extern \"C\" { fn signal }` (no new dependencies), `#[cfg(unix)]`-guarded. I proved the test has teeth by recompiling the pre-fix source and re-running the scenario: `C: signal(13)` / `pre-fix: exit(0)` / `post-fix: signal(13)`.\n\nWrite errors are still discarded, and that's correct — the closed-stdout case (`EBADF`) confirms C prints nothing and still exits 0.\n\n## Phase B/C — 21 tests, all comparing stdout bytes + stderr bytes + exit status\n\nBoth binaries driven as subprocesses; the Rust code is never loaded as a library. Status comparison renders signal deaths (`signal(13)`) distinctly from codes (`exit(0)`), so those two can never compare equal. Covered: empty/one-line/no-trailing-newline/1000-line/256 KiB/binary/closed stdin; 0/1/64/flag-like/empty/whitespace args; unusual `argv[0]`; stdout to file, closed, piped to a discarding reader, and a no-reader pipe; merged stderr; cleared env; five locales; 25 repeat runs.\n\n## Three harness defects I hit and recorded\n\nWorth flagging because each gave a *misleading* result rather than a clear failure:\n\n1. My SIGPIPE test first asserted `exit(141)` — wrong, because the helper script `exec`s, so the signal shows up directly as `signal(13)` with no shell to translate it.\n2. With the C binary absent, all 21 threads ran `cmake` in the same build dir concurrently and clobbered each other's temporaries → \"the C compiler is broken\", 21 failures unrelated to the translation. Now built once behind a `OnceLock`.\n3. A scratch reproduction omitted `mkfifo`, so stdout was a regular file, the write succeeded, and C looked like it exited 0 — hiding the exact mismatch I was chasing. The test now asserts `mkfifo` succeeded *and* that C really died of signal 13, so it can't degrade into a vacuous pass.\n\n## Phase D — gate\n\n- Both build with no errors; clean rebuild verified from scratch\n- `cargo test` **21 passed**, `cargo test --release` **21 passed**; 6 consecutive runs + `--test-threads=1` all stable, `cargo_exit=0`\n- 0 ignored, nothing skipped or disabled\n- `c_src/` sources unmodified (md5s match pre-work values; only the prescribed `c_src/build/` artifacts added)\n- `translation/ERRORS.md` records the mismatch table, the SIGPIPE root cause with reproduction, and the harness defects"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2781,"cache_read_input_tokens":68974,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2781,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool